Abstract = {Climate variability combined with rapid urbanization and population
   growth is increasing pressure on urban drainage systems, amplifying
   flood risks and associated impacts on public health and the urban
   environment. To support evidence-based decision making in this context,
   this study presents an optimal coverage assessment framework to evaluate
   the performance and resilience of grey-green and hybrid urban drainage
   systems under spatio-temporal rainfall variability. The proposed
   framework integrates reliability, resilience, and sustainability
   concepts to jointly assess system exposure to rainfall stressors and its
   hydraulic response. Natural threats are represented through design
   storms associated with multiple return periods, extreme rainfall events
   derived from continuous time series, and plausible rainfall extreme
   events, while system response is quantified using key hydraulic
   indicators obtained from hydrodynamic simulations, including runoff
   generation, flood volumes, and network surcharging behavior. A
   multi-objective optimization approach is applied to identify the optimal
   spatial extent of green stormwater management practices, explicitly
   accounting for performance gains and land-use constraints. The
   robustness of policy-relevant outcomes is ensured through a
   multi-criteria decision analysis that enables transparent comparison of
   alternative infrastructure configurations. The framework is demonstrated
   through its application to the urban drainage system of Quito (Ecuador),
   a representative case of rapidly growing cities in complex topographic
   and climatic settings. Results show that hybrid grey-green
   configurations consistently outperform conventional systems, achieving
   high resilience levels, particularly in flood mitigation, despite
   requiring a limited allocation of urban space to green infrastructure.
   Overall, the proposed framework offers a transferable and
   decision-oriented tool to guide urban planners and policymakers in
   prioritizing resilient and sustainable stormwater management strategies
   under climate uncertainty.},
